why_better:
It removes the serial link 1 -> J2 -> link 2 path, which loses 34 points of R1 on the champion (J2 zone 18.4, link 2 ribbon 16.1). W is held from two sides by two two-force couplers. So the moving joints over the rim are O14 force-only pins (~8 mm) instead of an O20 J2 hub (12.25 mm). Each elbow is confined to a fixed circle about its own base axis, and the sweep can place two such circles instead of one. Two of the three motors sit on S (MVP2). The pointing is triangulated: stiffer, with less cuff twist (R17). Kinematic screen (bench poses, 8 deg margins, no skin, no parallel-singularity check):
- crank 60 / coupler 120: 0.94-0.97 of the poses; W wdir(60,30) with u1 bvec(-60,40), u2 bvec(-40,40) gives 0.97, the champion's W 0.96;
- crank 60 / coupler 60 with bases on the anterolateral dome, bvec(140,40) and bvec(160,40): 0.95.

topology:
S -> J1 (ACTIVE, O20 hub, dome axis a) -> L1 (plate on the dome) -> spherical four-bar:
- L1 carries pins p1 (ACTIVE: rocker-1 angle) and p2 (passive);
- rocker 1 (arc lambda1) and rocker 2 (arc lambda2) are two-force members;
- L2 (the coupler) carries pins q1, q2 (passive).
L2 -> J3 (ACTIVE, O20 hub, arm-fixed axis W) -> arm-end plate -> neck to BOTTOM.

All axes pass through GH. 6 joints: 3 active (J1, p1, J3), 3 passive (p2, q1, q2). Rockers that cross give a polycentric joint whose coupler 'rolls' round the rim. Open rockers give a 'morphing link 2' that bends with the J2 angle. Option: a second four-bar in place of J3, with ground pins on the arm at the low-motion patch / BOTTOM saddle, so the deltoid-belly hub disappears.

main_risk:
Workspace before any skin check. My kinematic screen (bench poses, extension 25-125 deg, |det| > 0.15) gets 0.74 of all poses (clips 0.85) with legs bvec(-180,40) -> wdir(60,30), bvec(-30,40) -> wdir(100,-60) and bvec(150,80) -> wdir(115,90). But two of those arm axes are outside the rigid-cuff window (theta -20..+62), and one base sits on the medial trapezius, which bunches overhead. Re-screen with the window first; expect lower. Other risks:
- three lattices are a lot of thin metal over the cap;
- the lattices 'breathe' sideways (half-width delta up to ~15 deg when folded);
- 24 pins accumulate friction and backlash into the arm readout;
- a lattice near full stretch is very compliant and has poor sensing resolution;
- legs may cross each other (stacking costs height).

topology:
As fivebar-roll, but each elbow is a rolling-contact joint instead of a pin:
- each leg: S -> R_Ak (ACTIVE, O20 hub, dome axis u_k) -> crank k, ending in a convex edge disc (angular radius rho about c1k, fixed in the crank);
- that disc rolls without slip on coupler k's edge disc (radius rho about c2k), held by three crossed 0.1 mm steel or Ti flexure straps (Jacob's ladder); 1 DOF, no bearing, both link ends on ONE layer;
- coupler k -> R_C (passive) at the arm-fixed axis W.
Coupler 1 -> R_D (ACTIVE roll about W) -> arm plate -> neck to BOTTOM.

The joints at W and the base are real rolling bearings. 6 joints: 3 active, 3 passive (C plus two rolling elbows).

model_spec:
Subclass docs/concepts/models/spine-parked-j2-3r.py (same SLIM table, crowns, extras, declared_audit). Add params beta (30), az_c / el_c of the pivot axis (seed bvec(-159.9, 75.4); or build it as u1 tilted by beta about a normal turned phi = 330 about u1), qmax 75, m_stop 10, rad0 7.5.

build(B, F, p):
1. Axes. u1r = F.S(K.bvec(az, el)), p3 = F.HG(K.wdir(wd, wa)), c = F.S(K.bvec(az_c, el_c)), th = deg(arccos(u1r . p3)), lo = |a1 - a2| + m_stop, hi = a1 + a2 - m_stop.
2. Mode A (lo <= th <= hi): u1 = u1r, p2 = K.closure(u1r, p3, a1, a2, sigma, margin_deg = m_stop).
3. Mode B (th > hi) or C (th < lo): ths = hi or lo. For s = +-1: u1s, ok_s = K.closure(c, p3, beta, ths, s, margin_deg = 10); q0_s = K.axis_angle(c, u1r, u1s). Pick per pose the root of least |q0|, require |q0| <= qmax. Then p2 = K.closure(u1, p3, a1, a2, sigma, margin_deg = 0); angle(u1, p3) = ths, so J2 sits exactly on its stop.
4. ok = mode A ok, or (B/C ok and the pick valid).
5. q = [q0, axis_angle(u1, F.S([1, 0, 0]), p2), axis_angle(p2, u1, p3), axis_angle(p3, F.HG([0, -1, 0]), p2)], in the 10 fps continuity (K2 dense included: interpolated frames re-run the mode logic).

Members:
- hub0 = K.hub_member('hub0', c, 'R0', rad = 7.5) (S-fixed axis);
- hub1 at u1 (per pose, rad 10);
- hub2 at p2 (7.5);
- hub3 at p3 (7.5);
- rocker = K.strap_member('rocker', c, u1, ('R0', 'J1'), width 12, t 3, ramp_to = (('hub0', K.STACK), ('hub1', 0.0)));
- link1 u1 -> p2 (ramp_to hub1 STACK + 3.15 for the extra layer, hub2 0);
- link2 p2 -> p3 (over link1, the rocker and the neck);
- K.neck_member(F, p3, ...).

Checks:
- K6 on every pair, 1 mm radial (the rocker vs link 1 near J1 is the likely clash; give link 1 the upper layer);
- crowns and top faces (extras);
- the overhead split.
Report modes A/B/C per pose and q0 p5/p95.

